time required: 40 minutes
alternative_name: Truffle Mushroom Risotto
ingredient_count: 12
---

Heat @unsalted butter{2%tbsp} over medium heat in a #wide bottomed skillet{} or pan with at least 3 inch sides. Add @shallots{2%small}(finely minced) to the pan and cook for ~{2-3%minutes} until softened. Add @arborio rice{1 1/2%cups} and stir with a #wooden spoon{} to coat in the butter so that all the grains are shiny. Toast rice for about ~{1%minute}. Add @dry white wine{1/2%cup} and stir into the rice continuously.

Once the wine has been absorbed into the rice, add 1 cup of warm @chicken or vegetable broth{6%cups}(warmed) while constantly stirring the #pot{}. Once that broth has been absorbed, add another cup. A trick to tell when it's time to add more broth is if the bottom of the pan is exposed when you drag your spoon across the bottom. You will continue to add broth, 1 cup at a time while stirring until it has been absorbed into the rice and the rice is just slightly al dente.

While rice is cooking (and you're stirring), heat @unsalted butter{1%tbsp} and @neutral oil{1%tbsp} in a #cast iron{}. Add the @cremini mushrooms{12%oz}(quartered) and let them sear on one side until golden brown. Stir and cook for ~{2-3%minutes} more. Turn off the heat and season lightly with @salt{to taste} and @freshly cracked pepper{to taste}.

Once the rice has reached the desired doneness, turn off the heat. Stir in 2/3 of the cooked mushrooms, @black truffle puree{2%tbsp}, remaining @unsalted butter{5%tbsp}, @mascarpone cheese{1/2%cup}, and @parmesan{2/3%cup}(freshly grated). Stir until all the cheese and butter has melted. Season to taste with salt and pepper.

Ladle the risotto into #bowls{}. It should have the texture of slow-moving lava and not be a stiff ball. Top with extra mushrooms, @parmesan{for garnish}, and @chives{for garnish}(chopped).